AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) imposes stringent Quality of Service (QoS) requirements on communication networks, including low latency, high reliability, minimal packet loss, and predictable performance. Traditional network architectures face significant challenges in meeting these demands due to limited flexibility and static control mechanisms. Software-Defined Networking (SDN) offers a promising solution by enabling centralized control, programmability, and dynamic network management. However, the effectiveness of SDN in IoT environments strongly depends on the selection of an appropriate SDN controller. This paper proposes a systematic multi-criteria decision-making framework for selecting SDN controllers tailored to IoT networks with strict QoS constraints. The approach combines a comprehensive qualitative analysis of widely used non-proprietary SDN controllers with the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) and a quantitative mapping mechanism. Selection criteria are derived from IoT network requirements and include performance, scalability, load balancing, legacy network support, and implementation-related features. The results demonstrate that the proposed framework effectively identifies the most suitable SDN controller for QoS-sensitive IoT applications, providing a reusable and objective methodology for researchers and practitioners. ©2026 IEEE.
