AB  - Growing cyber threats are increasing the demand for multitenant security operations centers as a service (SOCaaS). The paper evaluates the suitability of selected open-source platforms as a technical basis for SOCaaS with an emphasis on academic and research environments, where openness, flexibility, and efficient management are key. Based on defined functional and technical criteria, we compare OpenStack, Apache CloudStack, Proxmox VE, and Kubernetes (concerning Kube-Virt). We mainly assess the completeness of IaaS functionality (VM, networks, storage, images, snapshots, API), architectural modularity, support for orchestration and automation (Heat, Terraform, Ansible, operators), performance and scalability, as well as reliability and high availability capabilities. The results show that OpenStack, thanks to its modular architecture, rich plug-in extensibility, and proven operational maturity, best meets the requirements for robust multitenant IaaS for SOCaaS. We recommend Apache CloudStack as a practical alternative with lower complexity. Proxmox VE is suitable for smaller internal or educational scenarios without strict scaling and multi-Tenancy requirements. Kubernetes with KubeVirt represents a promising path for moving to a cloud-native security service model, but it is not optimal as a primary IaaS for the current goal. The article provides methodological recommendations for platform selection and direction of further development of SOCaaS in an academic context.  © 2025 IEEE.
