AB  - In software development, the software architecture pattern has a direct impact on the number of system characteristics, including scalability, adaptability, and maintainability. Consequently, selecting an appropriate software architecture is a crucial decision. Such a decision is complex because of conflicting criteria and uncertainty in expert judgment. This study proposes a systematic, objective decision matrix for selecting optimal software architecture patterns by addressing uncertainty and bias in performance evaluation and criterion weighting. In this study, a Hybrid Entropy-Fuzzy Weighted Decision Matrix (HEF-WDM) is developed, in which entropy-based objective weights are integrated into the fuzzy decision process. This mechanism avoids early defuzzification, enforces data-driven objectivity, and maintains expert uncertainty. An academic portal case study that evaluates six software architecture patterns against eight criteria is used to validate the proposed decision matrix. Two benchmark methods, Fuzzy VIKOR with subjective weights and crisp Entropy-VIKOR, were applied to the same case study dataset. These results confirm that the proposed HEF-WDM provides a more methodologically consistent decision-support process that improves decision reliability, reduces subjective bias, and offers a rigorous, unbiased, and flexible method for selecting software architecture. The rank-agreement validation also confirmed that HEF-WDM was fully consistent with Crisp Entropy-VIKOR. The Spearman correlation, weighted Spearman correlation, and WS similarity coefficients were all equal to 1.0000. In comparison, HEF-WDM showed moderate agreement with Standard Fuzzy VIKOR, with corresponding values of 0.6571, 0.5592, and 0.5750. Beyond academic portals, the proposed decision matrix can be generalized to support the selection of suitable software architectures across different fields of system design. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
