AB  - Rapid urbanization requires precise, scalable, and data-driven approaches for identifying construction-ready land parcels. Traditional soil investigation and manual ground surveys are time-consuming and costly, making them inefficient for large-scale urban development. This paper presents an Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based vacant land detection and construction suitability analysis system for the Amaravati Capital Region using satellite imagery and Geographic Information Systems (GIS) data. Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery, along with derived spectral indices and soil and terrain datasets, are processed using the Google Earth Engine cloud platform. A Random Forest classifier is employed to identify vacant land parcels under consistent preprocessing and training conditions. A weighted multi-criteria evaluation model incorporating land use, soil properties, slope, flood risk, and accessibility is used to determine construction suitability zones. Experimental evaluation demonstrates high classification accuracy and improved efficiency compared to traditional assessment approaches. The proposed system provides a scalable and cloud-based decision-support framework for sustainable urban planning.  © 2026 IEEE.
