AB  - In this study, a hybrid neural network genetic algorithm (NNGA) architecture of a multicriteria process scheduling on federated clouds is suggested. Background Federated cloud computing gives resources a pool of multiple cloud providers, making it complicated to schedule processes depending on various cost and execution time objectives. Issue: The existing scheduling algorithms are unable to deal with costtime trade-offs and non-uniform resources and changing workloads. It requires a clever time and cost optimization model that finds the Paretooptimal solutions of costs and time whilst maintaining the QoS. Methodology: Deep neural networks (DNN) are used to predict workload and describe resources, and genetic algorithm (GA) is used to optimize resources to various purposes in the hybrid framework. We are using adaptive mutation rates, elitistic selection, and neural network crossover. Simulated datasets of Experimental WorkflowSim demonstrate cost reduction of 34.7 percent, 28.3 percent makespan reduction and 41.2 percent higher resources consumption than regular techniques. The convergence and variety of solutions of NNGA framework give costtime tradeoff efficiency. Conclusion: Federated clouds The hybrid model of multicriteria process scheduling is a powerful, scalable model, which can be deployed in order to deliver sustainable and efficient cloud computing in accordance with SDG 9 (Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure). © 2026 IEEE.
