AB  - The selection of an appropriate backend programming language is a pivotal decision in software engineering. It directly influences system performance, security, and long-term maintenance costs. This study addresses the limitations of intuitive decision-making and propose a mathematically rigorous framework by applying the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) and the Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS). We evaluate five popular backend languages: Python, Java, JavaScript (Node.js), PHP, and C#. The evaluation uses five carefully chosen criteria based on expert opinions: Performance & Scalability, Framework & Ecosystem, Security & Reliability, Industry Demand, and Ease of Learning & Usability. AHP is used to determine criterion weights through expert pairwise comparisons, resulting in Security & Reliability (35.2%) being identified as the most critical factor. AHP and TOPSIS rank the alternatives based on how close they are to ideal solutions. The analysis shows that Java is the top-ranked language with a TOPSIS score of 0.700 and an AHP weight percentage of 31.8%. The findings show that this approach effectively reduces subjective bias. It offers a flexible decision-support tool for selecting a backend programming language that matches real-world adoption trends and allows for prioritization based on specific projects.  © 2026 IEEE.
