TI  - A machine learning-based irrigation prediction model for cherry tomatoes in greenhouses: Leveraging optimal growth data for precision irrigation
PY  - 2025
T2  - Computers and Electronics in Agriculture
VL  - 237
C7  - 110558
DO  - 10.1016/j.compag.2025.110558
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105005267875?origin=resultslist
AB  - Intelligent precision irrigation management is crucial for achieving high-yield and high-quality crops in modern agriculture, particularly in greenhouse production. However, determining the optimal irrigation requirements for vegetables remains a key challenge in irrigation prediction model development. In this study, we conducted field experiments of cherry tomato under two light intensity treatments (100 % and 70 %), and four irrigation amount treatments [slightly dry (W1, 35 % ≤ RH (in substrate) < 45 %), moderate (W2, 45 % ≤ RH < 55 %), moist (W3, 55 % ≤ RH < 65 %), and excessively moist (W4, 65 % ≤ RH < 75 %)]. Analytic hierarchy process (AHP) and Technique for order preference by similarity to ideal solution (TOPSIS) methods were used to evaluate the growth effects of different treatment, and eight kinds of machine learning algorithms were used to establish prediction model of the optimal irrigation amount based on the data of vegetative growth and reproductive growth stage of cherry tomato, respectively. The results showed that light intensity is crucial in precise irrigation decision. Under sufficient light intensity condition, maintaining a moist substrate (55 % ≤ RH < 65 %) maximizes growth and quality of cherry tomato. In contrast, under weaker light intensity conditions, maintaining a slightly dry substrate (35 % ≤ RH < 45 %) enhances cherry tomato growth. The best performance was obtained using a combination of environmental temperature, humidity, and light intensity (both maximum and average values) as inputs, and XGBoost demonstrated the best prediction accuracy, achieving the highest R2 of 0.820 and 0.830 in the vegetative and reproductive growth stages, respectively, with the lowest RMSE. Based on model training, it was deployed on a cloud platform with irrigation control integrated via an STM32F103 microcontroller, and field validation trials showed that, compared to traditional irrigation methods, the irrigation model led to a 17.8 % increase in single fruit weight, with lycopene, soluble protein, and total soluble sugar contents rising by 23.1 %, 8.9 %, and 8.5 %, respectively, while water use efficiency improved by 20.7 %, demonstrating superior performance in fruit quality, yield, and resource utilization efficiency. This highlights the practical application of the system in precision agriculture and provides an efficient intelligent irrigation solution for greenhouse management. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
