TI  - Fair Automatic Labeling for Software Defined Network Controller Selection from Performance Metrics Using Analytic Hierarchy Process
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 International Conference on Current Research in Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, ICCRAIDS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICCRAIDS67816.2026.11519761
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041597608?origin=resultslist
AB  - In Software Defined Networking (SDN), controller selection determines network stability. However, contemporary labeling methods are based on subjective judgment, leading to mislabeling of the training set from the outset. Unlike previous manually selected weights, the Fair Automatic Labeling (FAL) method applies values calculated based on the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) according to three aspects: Compliance 0.724, Efficiency 0.193, and Stability 0.083, with a consistency check at 0.047. Each time controller fails 12 consecutive pings, its Compliance value will be zero. This means that a slow controller cannot achieve a high Efficiency value. Thirty separate Mininet simulations with five topology and three controllers (Ryu, ONOS, Floodlight), each running at bandwidths between 5 and 100 Mbps, were tested. In 21 out of 30 cases, the manual labels matched the actual values, while FAL correctly answered all 30 cases. Neither offers a 'one-size-fits-all' solution. Ryu excels in basic layouts with 60% of the choices, while ONOS dominates mesh settings with 30%. The score difference remains narrow; the smallest difference is 0.0008, and the spread, measured by standard deviation, is 0.0213. Therefore, using a single measure alone will not be sufficient unless multiple measures are combined. We did not test FAL on a real device. The conclusions apply only to emulators and Docker execution. © 2026 IEEE.
